          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following is the primary driver of the rising incidence of multiple</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following is the primary driver of the rising incidence of multiple pregnancies globally?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Advanced maternal age alone</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While advanced maternal age increases dizygotic twinning slightly, it is not the primary driver. The global rise in multiple pregnancies is principally driven by ART — ovulation induction (e.g. clomiphene, 6–8% twin rate) and IVF with multiple embryo transfer.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Assisted reproductive technology (ART)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. ART is the primary driver — ovulation induction with clomiphene carries a 6–8% twin rate, and IVF with double embryo transfer historically resulted in a 25–30% twin pregnancy rate.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Improved maternal nutrition</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Nutritional status has a minor influence on ovulation rates but does not account for the global rise in multiple pregnancies. ART, particularly ovulation induction and IVF, is the principal driver.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Increasing consanguinity rates</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Consanguinity does not increase twin incidence. The widespread use of ART — both ovulation induction and IVF with multiple embryo transfer — is the primary reason for the rising incidence.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is ART. Clomiphene-based ovulation induction carries a 6–8% twin rate, and IVF with double embryo transfer historically resulted in 25–30% twin pregnancies — far exceeding the natural twin rate of approximately 1 in 80 conceptions in India."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "ART — particularly ovulation induction and IVF with multiple embryo transfer — is the principal reason twin pregnancy incidence is rising worldwide."
                }
              ],
