          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following complications is specific to monochorionic twin pregnanci</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following complications is specific to monochorionic twin pregnancies due to shared placental circulation, rather than being common to all multiple pregnancies?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Anaemia is a general complication of all multiple pregnancies. The increased iron and folate demands of sustaining two fetuses cause it regardless of chorionicity — it does not require shared placental vasculature.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. TTTS is caused by unbalanced arteriovenous anastomoses in the shared monochorionic placenta, producing a donor–recipient haemodynamic imbalance. It cannot occur in dichorionic twins, who have entirely separate placentas.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Preterm labour</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Preterm labour is the most common complication of all multiple pregnancies, resulting from uterine overdistension regardless of chorionicity. It is not specific to monochorionic placentation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Post-partum haemorrhage</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>PPH complicates all multiple pregnancies because of the overdistended uterus and large placental site. It is a general complication, not one caused by shared placental vascular anastomoses.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is TTTS. Unlike anaemia, preterm labour, and PPH — which affect all multiple pregnancies — TTTS requires the shared placental vascular anastomoses found only in monochorionic twins."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "TTTS is pathognomonic of monochorionic placentation — the shared vascular anastomoses that enable unbalanced inter-twin blood flow cannot exist when each twin has a separate placenta."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
