          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A primigravida at her 12-week scan is found to have a twin pregnancy with a T-si</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A primigravida at her 12-week scan is found to have a twin pregnancy with a T-sign at the inter-twin membrane base. What is the most appropriate surveillance and delivery plan?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Growth scans every 4 weeks; planned delivery at 37–38 weeks</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is the protocol for dichorionic diamniotic (DCDA) twins, identified by a lambda sign. A T-sign indicates monochorionic diamniotic (MCDA) twins, which require significantly more intensive surveillance due to the risk of TTTS.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fortnightly scans from 16 weeks; planned delivery at 36–37 weeks</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A T-sign indicates MCDA twins. Per NICE NG137 guidelines, MCDA twins require fortnightly ultrasound from 16 weeks to detect TTTS early, with planned delivery at 36–37 weeks because the shared placenta ages faster than separate placentas.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Weekly scans from 20 weeks; planned delivery at 34 weeks</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Weekly scans from 20 weeks is not the standard protocol for any chorionicity type. MCDA twins (T-sign) require fortnightly surveillance from 16 weeks with planned delivery at 36–37 weeks.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inpatient admission from 28 weeks; planned delivery at 32–34 weeks</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is the protocol for monochorionic monoamniotic (MCMA) twins, who share both placenta and amniotic cavity with no inter-twin membrane. A T-sign indicates an inter-twin membrane is present — this is MCDA, not MCMA.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A T-sign identifies MCDA twins. The correct management is fortnightly ultrasound from 16 weeks to detect TTTS early, with planned delivery at 36–37 weeks. Compare: DCDA (lambda sign) = 4-weekly scans, deliver 37–38 weeks; MCMA (no membrane) = inpatient from 28 weeks, deliver 32–34 weeks."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "T-sign → MCDA twins → fortnightly USS from 16 weeks (to catch TTTS within days of onset) and planned delivery at 36–37 weeks. The chorionicity classification determines the entire surveillance schedule."
                }
              ],
